WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.635
Stop writing code

00:00:01.541 --> 00:00:02.449
and your reviews

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.062
get worse

00:00:03.582 --> 00:00:04.490
A new essay

00:00:04.301 --> 00:00:05.645
argues that developers

00:00:05.457 --> 00:00:06.247
who hand all

00:00:06.058 --> 00:00:06.942
their coding to

00:00:06.766 --> 00:00:08.110
AI agents and

00:00:08.004 --> 00:00:08.853
only review the

00:00:08.665 --> 00:00:10.008
output are slowly

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.082
losing their craft

00:00:11.614 --> 00:00:12.734
The author compares

00:00:12.546 --> 00:00:13.099
it to a

00:00:12.911 --> 00:00:14.349
guitarist who stops

00:00:14.161 --> 00:00:15.647
practicing and only

00:00:15.458 --> 00:00:16.731
directs other players

00:00:16.862 --> 00:00:17.781
or a writer

00:00:17.652 --> 00:00:18.713
who only reads

00:00:18.524 --> 00:00:19.856
and critiques mostly

00:00:19.668 --> 00:00:21.142
AI-generated prose

00:00:21.662 --> 00:00:22.735
His point isn't

00:00:22.546 --> 00:00:23.808
the short-term quality

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:24.739
of agent output

00:00:24.878 --> 00:00:25.692
It's that in

00:00:25.562 --> 00:00:26.670
every other field

00:00:26.777 --> 00:00:28.133
skills decay without

00:00:27.945 --> 00:00:29.347
practice and reviewing

00:00:29.159 --> 00:00:30.373
code well depends

00:00:30.185 --> 00:00:31.387
on actually writing

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:31.588
it

00:00:32.118 --> 00:00:33.132
Full story on

00:00:32.943 --> 00:00:33.839
the dashboard
